---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["what-is-full-time-equivalent"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A <strong>full-time equivalent</strong>, or FTE, converts a group of people
    working different hours into a single number expressed in full-time units.
    One person working a full week is 1.0 FTE. Two people each working half a
    full week are also 1.0 FTE combined. It lets an employer talk about
    workforce size in a way that is not distorted by part-time schedules.
  </p>
  <p>
    The basic formula is easy. The part that catches people out is that
    <strong>"full-time" is defined by the employer, and the US Affordable Care
    Act uses a different FTE method from the one most budgets use</strong>.
    Both are covered below.
  </p>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p><code>FTE = scheduled hours &divide; full-time hours for the same period</code></p>
  <p>
    "Full-time hours" is whatever the organisation defines as full-time —
    commonly 40 hours a week, sometimes 37.5 or 35. That choice changes the
    answer. An employee who works 37.5 hours a week is 1.0 FTE where full-time
    is 37.5 hours, but <code>37.5 &divide; 40 = 0.94</code> FTE where full-time
    is 40. Always check which yardstick a report is using.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>A small team, with full-time defined as 40 hours a week:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Hours / week</th><th>FTE</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Alex</td><td>40</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sam</td><td>40</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Priya</td><td>24</td><td>0.60</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Jordan</td><td>16</td><td>0.40</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>120</strong></td><td><strong>3.00</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Four people, but 3.0 FTE of work. Head count is 4; capacity is 3
    full-time-equivalent staff.
  </p>

  <h2>FTE to hours: a conversion table</h2>
  <p>
    Going the other way is just multiplication: weekly hours are
    <code>FTE &times; 40</code> and annual hours are <code>FTE &times; 2,080</code>
    (40 hours &times; 52 weeks), on a five-day week:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>FTE</th><th>Hours / week</th><th>Hours / year</th><th>Days / week</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0.25</td><td>10</td><td>520</td><td>1.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.50</td><td>20</td><td>1,040</td><td>2.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.60</td><td>24</td><td>1,248</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.75</td><td>30</td><td>1,560</td><td>3.75</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.80</td><td>32</td><td>1,664</td><td>4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.90</td><td>36</td><td>1,872</td><td>4.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1.00</td><td>40</td><td>2,080</td><td>5</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Annualised FTE</h2>
  <p>
    For budgeting and grants, FTE is often calculated over a year using total
    paid hours:
  </p>
  <p><code>annual FTE = total annual paid hours &divide; 2,080</code></p>
  <p>
    Someone who worked 1,560 hours in a year is <code>1560 &divide; 2080 =
    0.75</code> FTE. A role filled for only nine months of the year is
    <code>0.75</code> FTE even if it was full-time while occupied.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some bodies use a different yearly figure. The US federal government
    divides annual pay by <strong>2,087 hours</strong> rather than 2,080,
    because, averaged over a 28-year cycle, a calendar year contains 2,087
    work hours. On that basis 1,560 hours is 0.7475 FTE rather than 0.75.
    If you are reporting to an agency or funder, check which divisor their
    template uses.
  </p>

  <h3>Paid hours vs hours actually worked</h3>
  <p>
    The 2,080 figure counts every <em>paid</em> hour, including paid leave.
    Take a full-timer with 15 vacation days and 10 public holidays: that is 25
    days, or 200 hours, away from work. They are paid for 2,080 hours but
    work <code>2,080 &minus; 200 = 1,880</code>, which is 0.90 of the paid
    figure. Budgets usually use paid hours. Rotas and capacity plans, which
    ask how much work actually gets done, are better served by hours worked.
  </p>

  <h2>Pay for a partial-FTE role</h2>
  <p>
    A part-time version of a full-time role normally carries the same hourly
    rate, with fewer hours. If the full-time salary is $60,000 a year, the
    hourly rate is <code>60,000 &divide; 2,080 = $28.85</code>, and:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>FTE</th><th>Hours / year</th><th>Salary</th><th>Hourly rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0.50</td><td>1,040</td><td>$30,000</td><td>$28.85</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.60</td><td>1,248</td><td>$36,000</td><td>$28.85</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.80</td><td>1,664</td><td>$48,000</td><td>$28.85</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Salary scales with FTE, but total cost to the employer may not. Benefit
    costs such as a health premium can be a fixed amount per employee, so a
    0.6 FTE role can cost more than 60% of a 1.0 FTE role once benefits are
    included. The
    <a href="/calculators/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">Salary to Hourly
    Calculator</a> converts between the figures above.
  </p>

  <h2>The Affordable Care Act counts FTE differently</h2>
  <p>
    Under the ACA, a <strong>full-time employee</strong> is one who works on
    average at least 30 hours a week, or 130 hours in a calendar month. An
    employer with 50 or more full-time employees, <em>including</em>
    full-time equivalents, is an "applicable large employer" and must offer
    health coverage or face penalties. The IRS counts the FTEs like this:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>For each month, add up the hours of service of all employees who are <em>not</em> full-time, counting no more than 120 hours for any one employee.</li>
    <li>Divide that total by <strong>120</strong>. The result is the number of FTEs for the month.</li>
    <li>Add the month's full-time employees to its FTEs, do this for all 12 months of the prior year, and divide by 12.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The IRS's own example is Company Y: 40 full-time employees plus 20
    part-time employees who each work 60 hours a month. The part-timers add up
    to 1,200 hours, and <code>1,200 &divide; 120 = 10</code> FTEs. Average
    monthly total: <code>40 + 10 = 50</code>. Although Company Y has only 40
    full-time employees, it reaches the threshold of 50.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why the method matters: measured against a 40-hour week (about 173.3 hours
    a month), those same 20 part-timers would count as only 6.9 FTE, leaving
    the company at 46.9, under the line. Using the ACA's 120-hour divisor is
    what puts it over. This is a simplified summary for understanding the
    idea, not tax or legal advice. The IRS guidance covers the full rules,
    including seasonal workers and related companies.
  </p>

  <h2>Where FTE is used</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Budgeting and headcount planning</strong> — approving "2.5 FTE" for a team rather than a fixed number of people.</li>
    <li><strong>Grant and contract reporting</strong> — funders ask for effort in FTE, not names.</li>
    <li><strong>Benefits eligibility</strong> — the ACA employer-size test above.</li>
    <li><strong>Productivity metrics</strong> — output per FTE compares teams of different sizes fairly.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost modelling</strong> — a fully loaded cost per FTE (salary plus benefits and overhead) is a common planning figure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FTE vs head count vs hours</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Head count</strong> is the number of people, regardless of hours.</li>
    <li><strong>FTE</strong> is the number of full-time-equivalent schedules those people add up to.</li>
    <li><strong>Hours</strong> is the raw total. FTE is just hours divided by a full-time yardstick.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A department can have a head count of 10 and an FTE of 7.5 if several staff
    are part-time. Neither number is "right" — they answer different questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Common FTE mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the wrong full-time yardstick.</strong> 37.5 hours is 1.0
      FTE or 0.94 FTE depending on whether full-time is 37.5 or 40. Compare
      like with like.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mixing time periods.</strong> Divide monthly hours by monthly
      full-time hours, not weekly ones. 60 hours in a month &divide; 40 gives
      1.5, which is wrong; the correct figure is 60 &divide; 173.3 = 0.35.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Letting one person count as more than 1.0.</strong> Overtime
      doesn't create extra people. The ACA method caps each part-time employee
      at 120 hours a month in the FTE count, and many internal reports cap
      individuals at 1.0 as well. Check your own rules.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Treating FTE as a head count.</strong> 3.0 FTE might be three
      people or six. Report both numbers when both matter.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calculate it for your schedule</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/#per-week-month-year">working
    hours calculator</a> on the Hours Worked page takes hours per week, weeks per year, and PTO and returns
    hours per week, month, and year plus the full-time-equivalent figure. To
    see how paid time off changes annual hours, see
    <a href="/guides/how-to-calculate-pto-accrual/">how to calculate PTO
    accrual</a>, and for why 2,080 is the standard year, see
    <a href="/guides/how-many-work-hours-in-a-year/">how many work hours are in
    a year</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.irs.gov/affordable-care-act/employers/determining-if-an-employer-is-an-applicable-large-employer" rel="noopener">IRS — Determining if an employer is an applicable large employer (FTE calculation, 120-hour divisor, Company Y example)</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.irs.gov/affordable-care-act/employers/identifying-full-time-employees" rel="noopener">IRS — Identifying full-time employees (30 hours a week / 130 hours a month)</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.opm.gov/policy-data-oversight/pay-leave/pay-administration/fact-sheets/computing-hourly-rates-of-pay-using-the-2087-hour-divisor/" rel="noopener">US Office of Personnel Management — The 2,087-hour divisor</a></li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
